## Architecture

Split frontend/backend. The Next.js / React Native apps are **thin UI clients**; all stateful,
authoritative logic lives in the Rust backend. Next.js route handlers and server actions are at
most thin proxies to the Rust API, and the mobile app holds no business logic either.

```text
{{PROJECT_SLUG}}/
├── apps/
│   ├── web/        # Next.js frontend          (pnpm: {{PROJECT_SLUG}}-web,    port {{WEB_PORT}})
│   └── mobile/     # React Native (Expo) app   (pnpm: {{PROJECT_SLUG}}-mobile, Expo 8081)
├── packages/       # shared internal libs (optional)
├── services/
│   └── api/        # Rust (Axum) API + business logic                          (port {{API_PORT}})
├── docs/           # PRD.md, BRANDING.md
└── docker-compose.yml   # PostgreSQL (port {{DB_PORT}})
```

Convention: `apps/*` = deployed, `packages/*` = shared libs, `services/*` = hosted backends.
Rust dirs have no `package.json` and are skipped by pnpm.

## Stack

- **Web**: Next.js (App Router) + TypeScript + Tailwind + shadcn/ui
- **Mobile**: React Native via Expo (Expo Router) + TypeScript
- **Backend**: Rust (edition 2024), Axum, SQLx (async Postgres, no ORM)
- **DB**: PostgreSQL; schema = SQLx migrations in `services/api/migrations/`, applied on boot.

> Frameworks here may differ from your training data (Next.js, Expo, Axum, SQLx all move
> fast). Check current docs before writing framework code.

## Auth

- Email/password (bcrypt) + Sign in with Google, all in the Rust API, which issues the session
  JWT. The web app gets it as an HttpOnly `session` cookie and calls the API with
  `credentials: "include"`; page code never sees the token. The mobile app sends
  `X-Client: mobile`, receives the token in the response, keeps it in `expo-secure-store` and
  sends `Authorization: Bearer`.
- A Google sign-in links to an existing account by email only through the linking rule in
  `services/api/src/db/users.rs`: an unverified account loses its password and its sessions
  when it's linked.
- Bump `users.token_version` to revoke all of a user's sessions.
- CSRF: state-changing endpoints take JSON bodies only, and nothing changes state on GET.
- `apps/web/src/proxy.ts` is only an optimistic redirect; the API's 401 is the real gate.

## Dev commands

```bash
pnpm dev          # api + web (concurrently)
pnpm dev:all      # api + web + mobile
pnpm dev:web      # Next.js only (port {{WEB_PORT}})
pnpm dev:mobile   # Expo dev server
pnpm dev:api      # Rust API only (cargo run)
pnpm check        # type-check the web app
pnpm backend:test # cargo test
pnpm lint         # eslint on web
docker compose up -d --wait db   # Postgres
```

## Ports

| Service | Port |
|---|---|
| Next.js web | {{WEB_PORT}} |
| Expo dev server | 8081 |
| Rust API | {{API_PORT}} |
| PostgreSQL | {{DB_PORT}} |

## Environment variables

- `DATABASE_URL`: Postgres connection
- `JWT_SECRET`: signs session tokens; 32+ random bytes (`openssl rand -base64 48`), required
- `GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID` / `GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET` / `GOOGLE_REDIRECT_URI`: Sign in with Google
- `WEB_ORIGIN`: the web app's origin; the only CORS origin and where Google sign-in lands
- `COOKIE_DOMAIN`: production only, the parent domain when web and API are on sibling subdomains
- `NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL`: the API's URL for the web app
- `EXPO_PUBLIC_API_URL`: the API's URL for the mobile app
